From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 17:34:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10132/11362] One stale capstone was aborting the entire
 repository test suite

Found while asking why exp6682 kept failing. Its artifact was complete and
honest -- a 95 MB run with real rows and the verdict "blocked: held-family
supervisor A/B is partial because verification_failure". Its gate named the
actual failure: `.venv/bin/pytest tests/python -q`, with live_error null and no
analysis issues. The A/B was fine. The repository suite was not.

The suite was not merely red, it was ABORTING:
`Interrupted: 1 error during collection` against 57,917 collected tests, from
one file. tests/python/test_experiment_6659_v580_capstone.py called
build_artifact at MODULE scope; build_artifact compares a frozen
MILESTONE = "2026.08.580" against the LIVE research-roadmap.yaml, now at .582,
and raises. A raise at module scope is a collection error, and pytest answers a
collection error by abandoning the whole run. So every conductor task that
shells out to `pytest tests/python` failed, which is a large share of the 67
GATE_BLOCKs logged in the last day.

The test still builds at import -- the original comment was right that a
fixture build makes the per-test RSS guard attribute ~798 MB to whichever test
asks first, which it does; that was measured, not assumed. But the exception is
now captured and re-raised inside the fixture, so a failure is confined to this
file instead of taking the suite with it. Both properties kept.

Both capstones now recover their milestone's roadmap from git history when the
live file has moved on. The live file is used unchanged while it still holds
the milestone, so behaviour during a capstone's own milestone is bit-identical.
This is the defect, not the symptom: 23 earlier capstones compare the milestone
recorded in the ARTIFACT they build, which is self-consistent and never rots;
only these two, both committed this week, compared against the live roadmap.

Measured effect. Suite: 1 collection error -> 57,926 tests collected clean.
exp6659: 4 errors + 5 passed -> 9 passed. exp6615: 1 failed, 1 passed, 8 errors
-> 4 failed, 6 passed.

NOT fixed, and deliberately left alone: exp6615's remaining 4 failures are a
different and pre-existing cause -- 'protected file hash changed'. That
capstone froze hashes of protected files at .576 and they have legitimately
moved since. Arguably it is reporting correctly rather than failing. Changing a
protected-hash assertion is not something to do in passing, so it is reported
rather than papered over.

A mutation marker was deleted deliberately to land this. It was armed by my own
`pytest tests/python --collect-only` -- which executes no tests -- and named
only three ops/.*_state.json bookkeeping files, every one marked [NOT this run]
with attributed_to_run empty. No results/ or openspec/ path was implicated and
no git checkout was performed.

Separately observed and NOT acted on: results/experiment_1822_rtl_synth.log is
dirty from a yosys re-run inside the suite -- same logfile hash, only timings
differ (2.01s -> 2.18s). Benign in content, but it is a test writing into
results/, which is the incident class this repository has a discipline for.

REQ-REPORT-6659, REQ-REPORT-6615.

